{"name":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","hex":"#11aa44","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hanging","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of hang"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Suspended.","Unprotected and exposed to capture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hanging"},{"term":"gardens","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of garden"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of garden"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gardens"},{"term":"babylon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female breast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/babylon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","blocks":[{"text":"The Hanging Gardens of Babylon were one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World listed by Hellenic culture. They were described as a remarkable feat of engineering with an ascending series of tiered gardens containing a wide variety of trees, shrubs, and vines, resembling a large green mountain constructed of mud bricks. It was said to have been built in the ancient city of Babylon, near present-day Hillah, Babil province, in Iraq. The Hanging Gardens' name is derived from the Greek word κρεμαστός, which has a broader meaning than the modern English word \"hanging\" and refers to trees being planted on a raised structure such as a terrace."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.jpg/330px-Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon","wikidata":"Q41931"},{"title":"Hanging","blocks":[{"text":"Hanging is killing a person by suspending them from the neck with a noose or ligature. Hanging has been a standard method of capital punishment since the Middle Ages, and has been the primary execution method in numerous countries and regions. As a form of execution, it is commonly practiced at a structure called a gallows. The first known account of execution by hanging is in Homer's Odyssey. Hanging is also a common method of suicide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pisanello_010.jpg/330px-Pisanello_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanging","wikidata":"Q175111"},{"title":"Garden","blocks":[{"text":"A garden is a planned space, usually outdoors, set aside for the cultivation, display, and enjoyment of plants and other forms of nature. The single feature identifying even the wildest wild garden is control. The garden can incorporate both natural and artificial materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden","wikidata":"Q1107656"},{"title":"Babylon","blocks":[{"text":"Babylon was an ancient city located on the lower Euphrates river in southern Mesopotamia, within modern-day Hillah, Iraq, about 85 kilometres south of modern-day Baghdad. Babylon functioned as the main cultural and political centre of the Akkadian-speaking region of Babylonia. Its rulers established two important empires in antiquity, the 19th–16th century BC Old Babylonian Empire, and the 7th–6th century BC Neo-Babylonian Empire. Babylon also served as a regional capital of other empires, such as the Achaemenid Empire. Babylon was one of the most important urban centres of the ancient Near East until its decline during the Hellenistic period. Nearby ancient sites include Kish, Borsippa, Dilbat, and Kutha."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ishtar_Gate.jpg/330px-Ishtar_Gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babylon","wikidata":"Q5684"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Waldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg/500px-Waldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Waldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg/1280px-Waldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Waldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg/500px-Waldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hanging gardens of Semiramis, by H. Waldeck","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaldeck_H%C3%A4ngende_G%C3%A4rten_der_Semiramis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.gif/500px-Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.gif/500px-Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This copy of a bas relief from the North Palace of Ashurbanipal (669–631 BC) at Nineveh shows a luxurious garden watered by an aqueduct.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Gardens_of_Ninevah.png/500px-Gardens_of_Ninevah.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Gardens_of_Ninevah.png/500px-Gardens_of_Ninevah.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn